<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/10/style-generator-header-rb.7539.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>
<p>I spent some time this week making the <a href="https://markedapp.com/style-generator/">Style Generator</a> less fragile. If you&rsquo;ve ever imported a theme and watched the preview turn into a pale imitation of the original, this is the fix.</p>

<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/10/marked-style-generator-header-800.jpg"></p>

<h3 id="the-parser-used-to-give-up-early">The parser used to give up early</h3>

<p>The old splitter was basically &ldquo;find the first brace and call it a rule.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s fine until a stylesheet does something anything other than ordinary.</p>

<p>A CriticMarkup closer used to end the rule in the middle of the string:</p>

<div class="language-css highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="nt">span</span><span class="nc">.critic.comment</span><span class="nd">::after</span> <span class="p">{</span>
  <span class="nl">content</span><span class="p">:</span> <span class="s1">" &lt;&lt;}"</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="p">}</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>Google Fonts imports had a similar problem. A family list has semicolons inside the URL, and the parser treated the first one as the end of the line. Nested <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">@media</code> blocks got clipped at the first inner rule, so the rest of the query spilled out as broken CSS.</p>

<p>It now reads strings and nested blocks the way a stylesheet actually works. A brace inside quotes stays inside the quotes. A media query keeps the rules that belong to it.</p>

<h3 id="importing-a-theme-keeps-what-you-wrote">Importing a theme keeps what you wrote</h3>

<p>Opening a style for editing used to copy a few colors into the form and throw the rest away. Variable colors were the worst consequence of this. A headline written as <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">color: var(--coral)</code> got stripped, the form never received a real color, and the preview fell back to a default.</p>

<p>Custom properties had a second problem. The preview scopes rules under <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#wrapper</code>, which turned <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">:root</code> into a selector that never matched. The palette was defined and then immediately unreachable. Fonts went with it. One <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">@import</code> that listed Syne, Hanken Grotesk, and IBM Plex Mono kept only the first family, so the display face never loaded.</p>

<p>A page title could shrink for a sillier reason. The real <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">h1</code> might be <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">2.7rem</code>, and a table-of-contents label further down might be <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">0.72rem</code>. The importer treated that later label as the page heading.</p>

<p>Literal colors still land in the controls, so you can tweak a hex value from the sidebar. Anything the form can&rsquo;t represent stays in the custom CSS and shows up in the preview: <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">var()</code>, a header-cell color that isn&rsquo;t the body-cell color, the real <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">h1</code> size, and every family in the font import.</p>

<h3 id="make-one-yours">Make one yours</h3>

<p>The <a href="https://markedapp.com/styles/">style gallery</a> is the easiest way in. Open a style, hit Customize, and it loads in the <a href="https://markedapp.com/style-generator/">Style Generator</a> as a copy. Change the type, the palette, the spacing, then save it as your own.</p>

<p>You can also steal and tweak a style from your own blog or favorite site using Marked&rsquo;s Style Stealer, and then clicking the edit button in the Style Manager.</p>

<p>Styles you build in the generator aren&rsquo;t limited to Marked. nvUltra and MultiMarkdow Composer can work with these themes, and nvALT&rsquo;s Markdown preview is <a href="https://brettterpstra.com/2026/10/27/nvalt-gets-themeable/">themeable now</a>, so a stylesheet from the Style Generator works there too.</p>

